Core OOP Fundamentals — Java Interview Notes

1. What is OOP? Why use it?
Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming approach that organizes code around objects containing data and behavior.
- Why use it? Code reusability, modularity, maintainability, and easier testing.
- Four pillars: Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, and Abstraction.

2. Class vs Object
- Class: Blueprint defining fields and methods.
- Object: Instance of a class created at runtime.
class Car {
    String color;
    void drive() { System.out.println("Driving"); }
}

Car c = new Car();


3. State, Behavior, and Identity

- State: Data stored in fields, e.g., color = "Red".
- Behavior: Actions performed by methods, e.g., drive().
- Identity: Distinguishes one object from another, even if their data is identical.
4. Instance vs Local vs Static Variables

- Instance variable: Belongs to an object; each object has its own copy.
- Local variable: Declared inside a method or block; must be initialized before use.
- Static variable: Belongs to the class; shared among its instances.
class Employee {
    String name;                 // Instance
    static String company;       // Static

    void work() {
        int hours = 8;           // Local
    }
}


5. Constructors and Object Initialization

A constructor initializes an object when it is created using new. It has the same name as the class and no return type.
class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}


6. Default vs Parameterized Constructor

- Default constructor: Compiler-provided, no-argument constructor when you declare no constructors.
- Parameterized constructor: Accepts arguments to initialize an object.
- If you declare any constructor, Java does not automatically provide the no-argument constructor.
Student() { }                    // No-argument constructor
Student(String name) { }         // Parameterized constructor


Interview tip: A default constructor is specifically compiler-generated; a manually written no-argument constructor is not technically a default constructor.

7. Constructor Chaining Using this() and super()
- this() calls another constructor in the same class.
- super() calls a constructor of the immediate parent class.
- In traditional Java syntax, either must be the first constructor statement.
- You cannot use both as separate first statements in the same constructor.
class Animal {
    Animal(String name) { }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        this("Buddy");
    }

    Dog(String name) {
        super(name);
    }
}


8. Initialization Order
For a typical Java object, the order is:
1. Static fields and static blocks, once per class initialization.
2. Instance fields and instance initializer blocks, in textual order.
3. Parent-class instance initialization and constructor execution before the child constructor body.
4. Child-class instance initialization and constructor body.
Remember: The superclass constructor executes before the subclass constructor body. Static initialization follows class initialization order.
9. Object References, Creation, and Assignment
- new creates an object and returns a reference to it.
- A reference variable holds the reference, not the object itself.
- Assigning one reference to another makes both refer to the same object.
Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = s1;

s2.name = "Amit";
System.out.println(s1.name); // Amit


Both variables refer to the same object. Changing the object through either reference is visible through the other.
10. Procedural vs Object-Oriented Programming
Procedural programming	Object-oriented programming
Organized around functions	Organized around objects
Data and functions are often separate	Data and behavior are grouped together
Focuses on procedures and steps	Focuses on objects and their interactions
Example: C	Example: Java

Interview point: Java is primarily object-oriented, but it also supports primitive types and static methods, so not everything in Java is an object.
